=== JUDGMENT ON THE MERITS (not a rubber stamp of the proposing auditors) ===

I re-derived the question rather than assuming round 1 was right.

Meaning: English "Actions for {name}" is a neutral noun phrase naming the asset whose
actions menu opens — not a command to act on the name (context sidecar). The fr value
keeps that: "Actions pour <name>" is the "for <asset>" relation, the same relation the
English states. No omission, addition, negation or polarity change; no destructive,
permission or error wording here.

Why "pour" and not the previous "sur": both prepositions are grammatical French with a
bare inserted name, so this is not a meaning defect — round 1 classified it MAJOR on
terminology/consistency grounds and that is the honest label for it:
  - "pour" is the decision the catalogue already made for this exact English sentence:
    four shipped files carry "Actions pour {name}", plus "Actions pour {displayName}"
    and "Actions pour \"{title}\"". Before the change this key was the one outlier.
  - "sur" in this corpus is the article-bearing generic form only ("Actions sur le
    fichier", "Actions sur l’image", "Actions sur l’élément", "Actions sur le message")
    — 0 values use "Actions sur <placeholder>". Using it on the named path would ask
    the reader to hold two prepositions for one concept with no rule behind it.
  - The gloss is "one term, one decision" (glossary § intro), and the sibling
    name-directed labels ("Autres actions pour {name}", "Actions de fichier pour {name}",
    "Actions de l’onglet pour {title}") are all "pour".
  - Fit for arbitrary inserted text: "pour" takes no article, no gender agreement and no
    elision (unlike "de", which would need "d’" before a vowel-initial name). Any file or
    image display name — spaces, «guillemets», apostrophes, dots, CJK, emoji — slots in
    without a grammar change. "Actions pour Ébauche finale.png" needs nothing extra.

Register and typography: sentence case with a capital "Actions", no trailing punctuation,
plain ASCII, straight spaces. Nothing in the fr guide applies here beyond that: no
«…» quoting (the English does not quote the name; the sibling {name} keys do not either),
no espace fine before a punctuation mark because there is none, no digits, no plural.

Fit: the sidecar carries no maxCells for this namespace (grep -c maxCells = 0 on the whole
file), and the string is an aria-label, never a truncating container. 19 characters, one
more than the previous value and shorter than the shipped generic neighbours
("Actions sur le fichier", 22). No overflow or wide-glyph risk (pure ASCII).

Consistency with the prior round's other certified keys: the changed value does not
contradict them. Its counterpart in the same component pair, the generic
ui.chat.image_actions.actions = "Actions sur l’image" (certified clean last round), still
serves the unnamed fallback and the tooltip, and that generic/named split is exactly what
the context sidecar prescribes. So the pair now reads "Actions sur l’image" (unlabelled
image) vs "Actions pour photo.png" (named) — two roles, one rule each, no contradiction.
The prior round's other nit (ui.chat.audio.position_label) lives in a namespace this delta
does not touch and is not re-audited here.

Second independent pass: this key sits in a common-actions group, so I re-read the value
and its neighbours a second time after the mechanical sweep, deliberately asking whether
"pour" could be the worse choice (benefactive misreading? article missing? elision?) and
whether an alternative such as "Actions de {name}", "{name} : actions" or
"Plus d’actions pour {name}" would serve better. None of them survives the corpus check
(the first two have no precedent here and would split the invariant; the third adds words
the English does not have), so the second pass agreed with the first. No separate second
auditor/session was available or requested for this delta; that limitation is recorded
rather than glossed over.

OUTCOME: no finding. F1 of the prior round is remediated correctly, by a value that is
grammatical for arbitrary inserted names, native in register, and consistent with every
other rendering of the same English sentence. Nothing gates.

